To avoid SQL injection, you should use prepared statements or parameterized queries instead of building SQL queries using string concatenation. Here's an example of how to modify the 'getPaymentDetails' method to use a prepared statement:

```php
public function getPaymentDetails($input) {
    $identifier = strstr($input, '_', true) ?: $input;
    $stmt = $this->pdo->prepare("SELECT * FROM payments WHERE transaction_id LIKE :identifier");
    $stmt->bindValue(':identifier', $identifier . '%', PDO::PARAM_STR);
    $stmt->execute();
    $records =  $stmt->fetchAll(PDO::FETCH_ASSOC);
    $this->pdo = null;
    return $records;
}
```

This way, the user input is properly sanitized and escaped, preventing SQL injection attacks.

